{"id":"20391351-406b-4c6a-856e-b443b513b563","object":"chat.completion","created":1784838303,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    \\n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        s = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[s] = symbol_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    # Sort candidates: depth descending, then id ascending\\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    removal_order = []\\n    \\n    def collect_subtree(node_id, retained):\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Return list of node ids in retained subtree rooted at node_id.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        if node_id not in retained:\\n            return []\\n        result = [node_id]\\n        for child in children.get(node_id, []):\\n            result.extend(collect_subtree(child, retained))\\n        return result\\n    \\n    for node in candidates:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any child is still retained\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        # Compute lost symbol counts if we remove this subtree\\n        subtree_nodes = collect_subtree(nid, retained)\\n        lost_counts = {}\\n        for sid in subtree_nodes:\\n            s = node_map[sid]['symbol']\\n            lost_counts[s] = lost_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n        # Check required symbols remain\\n        feasible = True\\n        for sym in required:\\n            current = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0)\\n            lost = lost_counts.get(sym, 0)\\n            if current - lost <= 0:\\n                feasible = False\\n                break\\n        if not feasible:\\n            continue\\n        # Perform removal\\n        for sym, cnt in lost_counts.items():\\n            symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) - cnt\\n        for sid in subtree_nodes:\\n            retained.discard(sid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    \\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained': sorted_retained, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. The user provided a fixture payload with cases, but the actual task is to implement `solve(case)` that returns a JSON-serializable object. The task description: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" on a parse tree. Input: nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols. We need to reduce the tree by removing nodes that are removable, but preserving required symbols and ensuring grammar validity (though not specified grammar, maybe just that root and required symbols remain). We need to consider deeper nodes before shallower, never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nThe output format: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So likely a dict with two keys: \"retained\": list of node IDs sorted, and \"removal_order\": list of node IDs in order they were removed.\n\nWe must implement `solve(case)` where case is a dict with \"payload\" containing \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". The function must return a JSON-serializable object.\n\nWe need to process: build tree from nodes. Identify nodes that are removable (removable == true). Then we need to decide which to remove. The algorithm: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id (stable tie break). For each node in that order, if it is removable, and if all required symbols are still represented after removing it (i.e., the subtree rooted at that node does not contain any required symbol that would be lost if removed? Actually \"all required symbols remain represented\" means in the whole tree after removal, each required symbol appears at least once among retained nodes. Since required symbols are symbols, not nodes. So we need to check that after removing that node (and its descendants), each required symbol still exists among remaining nodes. And also that the node has no retained children (i.e., children that are themselves retained? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if any child of this node is not removed (i.e., retained), then we cannot remove the parent. So we must only remove leaf nodes? Or nodes whose all children have been removed? Since we process deeper first, children will be considered before parents. So if we remove a child first, then parent may become leaf. So algorithm: process nodes depth descending. For each node, if removable, and if all required symbols still present after removal (including symbols in its descendants that are already removed? Actually removal removes node and all its descendants, but if some descendants were already removed, they are gone. So we need to check current set of nodes to be retained. We can simulate: start with all nodes. For each candidate in order, check if removing it (and its descendants that are still present) would cause any required symbol to be missing. Also check that it has no children that are still retained (i.e., children not yet removed). Since we process depth descending, children are deeper, so they will be processed before parent. So when we consider parent, its children that are removable might have been removed already. But if a child is not removable, it will remain, so parent cannot be removed because child is retained. So we need to check if all children are either already removed or are not retained? Actually \"node with retained children\" means any child that is still in the retained set. So if any child is still retained, we cannot remove parent. So we can keep a set of retained nodes, initially all nodes. As we decide to remove a node, we remove it from retained. Then later when considering its parent, we check if any child is in retained. So that works.\n\nAlso check \"all required symbols remain represented\". We need to keep track of which symbols are present. After removal, we can recompute or maintain a set of symbols from retained nodes. But careful: a required symbol might appear multiple times; we need at least one occurrence. So we can maintain a count of occurrences per symbol. Initially, count from all nodes. When we remove a node, we decrement count for its symbol. But we also need to decrement for its descendants? Actually removing a node removes its whole subtree. So we need to decrement counts for all nodes in that subtree that are still retained. Since we process deeper first, some descendants may already be removed, so we only decrement for those that are still in retained. So we need to know the subtree of the node among currently retained nodes. Simpler: after tentative removal, check if any required symbol count becomes zero. We can compute by traversing the subtree (using parent relationships) and counting symbols among retained nodes that are descendants of this node. But we can maintain a set of retained nodes and a symbol count map. When we consider removal of a node, we need to know the set of retained descendants (including itself). We can precompute subtree nodes? But the tree changes as we remove. Alternatively, we can iterate over all retained nodes at each step? That might be okay given small size (fixtures have 7 nodes). But we need a general solution.\n\nBetter approach: Build adjacency list (children). Start with all nodes in retained set. Compute symbol counts. Then process nodes in order (depth descending, then id ascending). For each node, if it is removable, and if it has no retained children (i.e., all children are either not retained? Actually check if any child is in retained set - if yes, skip). Then simulate removal: subtract counts for this node and all retained descendants. If after subtraction any required symbol count goes to zero, then skip (don't remove). Else, remove: update retained set, update counts, record removal order. Then continue.\n\nBut we need to be careful: when we subtract counts for descendants, we need to consider only those that are still retained. Since we process deeper first, it's likely that many descendants have been removed already. So we need to compute the subtree of retained nodes rooted at this node. We can do a DFS from the node, but only following children that are in retained set.\n\nImplementation steps in solve:\n- Parse case: payload dict with nodes list and required_symbols list.\n- Build a dict mapping node id to node object. Also build children dict: map parent id to list of child ids.\n- Identify root: node with parent None.\n- Initialize retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Build initial symbol_counts: count occurrences of each symbol among all nodes.\n- required_set = set(required_symbols).\n- Order nodes: sort by depth descending, then by id ascending (to break ties). That list is candidates.\n- removal_order = []\n- For each candidate in candidates:\n    if candidate id not in retained_ids: continue (already removed)\n    node = nodes[candidate]\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    # check if any child is retained\n    children = children_dict.get(candidate, [])\n    has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained_ids for child_id in children)\n    if has_retained_child: continue\n    # compute symbol counts that would be lost if remove this node and its retained descendants\n    lost_counts = {}\n    # DFS to collect all retained descendants (including itself)\n    def collect(node_id):\n        if node_id not in retained_ids:\n            return\n        symbol = nodes[node_id]['symbol']\n        lost_counts[symbol] = lost_counts.get(symbol, 0) + 1\n        for child in children_dict.get(node_id, []):\n            collect(child)\n    collect(candidate)\n    # check if any required symbol would become zero\n    feasible = True\n    for sym in required_set:\n        current_count = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0)\n        lost = lost_counts.get(sym, 0)\n        if current_count - lost <= 0:\n            feasible = False\n            break\n    if not feasible:\n        continue\n    # proceed with removal\n    # update symbol_counts\n    for sym, cnt in lost_counts.items():\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) - cnt\n    # remove all nodes in lost_counts? Actually we need to remove the node and its retained descendants from retained_ids\n    # We have collected the set of node ids in the subtree (via DFS). We can store them in a list.\n    removed_nodes = []\n    # We'll collect during DFS again or store\n    # Let's do DFS to get list of ids to remove\n    def collect_ids(node_id):\n        if node_id not in retained_ids:\n            return []\n        res = [node_id]\n        for child in children_dict.get(node_id, []):\n            res.extend(collect_ids(child))\n        return res\n    to_remove = collect_ids(candidate)\n    for rid in to_remove:\n        retained_ids.discard(rid)\n    removal_order.append(candidate)\n    # Note: we removed the whole subtree, but note that children might have been processed already? If they were processed, they might have been removed earlier. But our collect_ids only includes those still retained, so it's fine.\n- After loop, sort retained_ids (since we need sorted retained IDs). Since retained_ids is set, sort to list.\n- Return {\"retained\": sorted(retained_ids), \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable, required_symbols may include root. Also required_symbols may include symbols that appear multiple times. Need to ensure we don't remove if it would eliminate the last occurrence.\n\nNote: The task says \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\" but not defined; we assume the only constraints given. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" refers to order we process.\n\nTesting with fixture case 0: nodes: root(symbol root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2), n6(s0). Required: root, s1. Removable: n2, n4, n6. Depth: root0, n1,n2,n3 depth1, n4,n5,n6 depth2. Order: depth2 first: n4(id n4), n5(id n5), n6(id n6). But n5 is not removable. So candidates: n4, n6 (depth2), then depth1: n2, n3 not removable, n1 not removable. Then root depth0.\n\nProcess n4: removable? yes. Children? none. Check retained descendants: itself only. Symbol s1. Current count of s1: from nodes: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2), n6(s0). So s1 count = 2 (n1 and n4). After removal, count becomes 1, still >=1. So can remove. Remove n4. retained becomes all except n4. symbol counts: s1 becomes 1.\n\nProcess n6: removable? yes. Children? none. Symbol s0. Required symbols: root, s1. s0 not required, so removal does not affect required. So can remove. Remove n6.\n\nNow depth1: n2 is removable? Yes. Children: n4 (already removed) so no retained children. Check retained descendants: itself (s2) and any retained children? n4 removed, so only itself. s2 not required. So can remove. Remove n2.\n\nThen n1 and n3 not removable. root not removable. So retained: root, n1, n3, n5? Wait n5 is still there? n5 is depth2, parent n3, not removable. It was not removed. So retained includes n5. Also n1, n3, root. Sorted IDs: n1, n3, n5, root? Actually root id is \"root\". Sorting lexicographically: \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\". Removal order: n4, n6, n2. That seems plausible.\n\nBut need to verify that when removing n2, we didn't lose any required symbol after removing its subtree? Its subtree originally had n4 (s1) but n4 already removed, so no issue.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow implement in Python. We'll define solve(case) that takes case dict (which includes \"payload\"). We'll access case[\"payload\"] and then nodes, required_symbols.\n\nWe need to return JSON-serializable. We'll use standard dict.\n\nAlso note that the task says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sorting by depth descending, then id ascending.\n\nWe'll implement as described.\n\nNow output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source code. We must escape newlines and quotes for JSON. We'll write the code as a multi-line string, then use json.dumps to escape. But we need to produce the JSON string manually. Better to write the Python code in a variable and then use json.dumps to generate the JSON string. However we are generating the response, we can construct the JSON manually by escaping. Since the response is short, we can do it carefully.\n\nWe'll write a Python script that itself is the implementation. But the task is to provide the implementation string. So we need to write a valid Python function solve(case). We'll include imports (no external). We'll use only standard libraries.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    ...\"}\n\nMake sure to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\" etc.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The nodes list may not be sorted. We need to build children dict. Also root may not be first. We'll parse.\n\nWe'll also ensure we handle case where node id may be string. Sorting by id ascending works lexicographically if IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. That's fine.\n\nWe'll need to be careful about recursion depth? Tree depth small.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1734,"completion_tokens":3619,"total_tokens":5353,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2992},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":70},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}